# What the keys file looks like -- the file KINGFISHER_MODEL_KEYS_FILE names.
#
# Copy it out of this workspace before writing a key in it. On macOS the agent's
# shell can read the workspace, so a key left here is one it can read; under your
# home folder and outside the workspace, the sandbox hides it:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/kingfisher
#   cp keys.yaml.example ~/.config/kingfisher/keys.yaml
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/kingfisher/keys.yaml
#
# and in .env:
#
#   KINGFISHER_MODEL_KEYS_FILE=~/.config/kingfisher/keys.yaml
#
# One line per entry. The name on the left is what an endpoint's `key:` says in
# models.yaml; the value is the key, as text -- quote one YAML would read as a
# number. `kingfisher doctor` says where each endpoint's key came from, and warns
# when this file sits where the agent's shell or another user can read it.

# minimax: sk-...
# openai: sk-...
